1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Alright, let's talk about our first element for fluid power systems, the kind of long pipe or pressure momentum.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 Probably not a great one to lead with, I guess, because first of all we don't see this very often.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 And second of all, the kind of derivation of the CCRs and SSR is a little bit gross.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 So sorry to kind of kick things off with such an unexciting and probably irrelevant element.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:33,000
 But it's worth talking about in this order so that we maintain the same structure I guess between this electrical systems, rotating systems and mechanical systems.

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 So the generalized inertia for fluid power systems is kind of the momentum of the massive fluid that's in a pipe.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 And the reason I say this is irrelevant is because this momentum only really becomes appreciable when you have really, really long pipes or really, really thin pipes.

8
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 And here's why. The way we talk about the momentum of a fluid in a pipe comes about from a reasonably long and uninteresting derivation that's in the lecture some reason.

9
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 The textbook, if you're interested in reading, but I won't repeat here for the sake of your sanity.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:17,000
 Needless to say, you get something like this where the pressure momentum, kind of the state that energy variable we're interested in, is a function of kind of the density of the fluid, the length of the pipe and the cross-sectional area of the pipe.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 And only really becomes appreciable if the density of the fluid is massive. If the length of the pipe is massive, more of the cross-sectional area is tiny.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,000
 Otherwise it's something that's just not worth considering. And that's going to be the case for many of our fluid power systems.

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 Because we're ordinarily going to be modeling kind of hydraulic circuits, maybe the kinds of things that you use to drive actuators or bobcats or whatever, we won't have to deal with this pressure momentum.

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 Where it might be applicable is something like, I don't know, a pipe delivering really, really high pressure fluid from a dam down to a kind of town or something.

15
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 And I think we have a problem similar to that that we'll do later this week. But in the vast majority of cases, you're not going to come across this.

16
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 That being said, we should kind of talk about the CCRs to be thorough.

17
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 So the first kind of CCR for a long pipe is that the pressure momentum is kind of quantity, we call the inertia of the pipe.

18
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 Really, it's just a kind of conglomerate of all of its properties, the density of the fluid, its length and cross-sectional area.

19
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 Multiply by the flow rate of fluid going through that pipe.

20
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 This obviously looks a lot like the kind of physical momentum we've seen for masses before, right? P equals MV.

21
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 Or the electrical equivalent lambda equals Li. And this is for a reason, these things are the same under the hood, right?

22
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 But where it's also similar is in the derivative. Just like with a mass, if you took the derivative of momentum, you got net force.

23
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 Or with a flux, if you took the derivative, you got kind of voltage. If you take the derivative of a pressure momentum, if you take the derivative of a fluid power momentum, you get the pressure change across that element across that long pipe.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:04,000
 Again, in most cases, you're probably not going to see this element crop up. It kind of goes against our fundamental assumptions that we make in the modeling of fluid power systems, right? High pressures, low flows,

25
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 incompressible fluids, we're generally modeling hydraulic circuits. You don't tend to have runs where this is appreciable.

26
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 But if it does, we've now got the CCRs and the tools to help you get the job done.

27
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
 All right, let's go and talk about maybe some more relevant elements.
